  ('economics/quantitative-foundations/probability-trees-and-conditional-expectations/expected-values', 'economics', 'economics/quantitative-foundations/probability-trees-and-conditional-expectations', 'Expected Values', 'expected-values', 'Calculate expected values, variances, and standard deviations, and apply them to investment problems.', 2, 2),
  ('economics/quantitative-foundations/probability-trees-and-conditional-expectations/bayes-formula', 'economics', 'economics/quantitative-foundations/probability-trees-and-conditional-expectations', 'Bayes’ Formula', 'bayes-formula', 'Calculate and interpret an updated (posterior) probability in an investment setting using Bayes’ formula.', 3, 2),
  ('economics/quantitative-foundations/rates-and-returns/core-concepts', 'economics', 'economics/quantitative-foundations/rates-and-returns', 'Core Concepts', 'core-concepts', 'What an interest rate is, and the three equivalent ways to read it: as a required rate of return, as a discount rate used to bring future cash flows to the present, and as the opportunity cost of current consumption. An interest rate as the sum of a real risk-free rate plus premiums that compensate investors for bearing distinct types of risk — inflation, default, liquidity, and maturity risk. And how a return is measured over time, including how to calculate it.', 0, 2),
  ('economics/quantitative-foundations/rates-and-returns/interest-rates-and-return-measurement', 'economics', 'economics/quantitative-foundations/rates-and-returns', 'Interest Rates and Return Measurement', 'interest-rates-and-return-measurement', '', 1, 2),
  ('economics/quantitative-foundations/rates-and-returns/time-weighted-and-money-weighted-returns', 'economics', 'economics/quantitative-foundations/rates-and-returns', 'Time-Weighted and Money-Weighted Returns', 'time-weighted-and-money-weighted-returns', 'The money-weighted rate of return (the IRR of a portfolio''s cash flows) and the time-weighted rate of return; how they differ and when each is appropriate; and how to evaluate and compare portfolio performance using these measures — including why time-weighted return is preferred for judging a manager.', 2, 2),
